    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d264">

                <pb facs="0506" n="386" sameAs="frus:frus1932v01/pg386"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 Steering Committee/146: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        American Delegate (Wilson)</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-11-15T12:00:00-05:00">November 15,
                            1932—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>245. Your 452, November 14, 6 p.m. Your understanding that you should oppose
                    suggestions (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>), (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)
                    and (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>) is correct.</p>
                <p>You should support the proposal that any system of publicity of manufacture
                    should apply also to material in stock, private and state.</p>
                <p>The convention of 1925 is still before the Senate. Therefore what we contemplated
                    was not resubmission of that treaty but an effort to secure favorable action
                    upon it by the Senate. Such effort, if made, should be made as soon as possible
                    after Congress reconvenes. For our guidance in this matter, please keep us fully
                    informed of all developments as they occur.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
